Lab 10: Configuring Distributed Transactions in Camel
Objectives
By the end of this lab, you will be able to:

Understand the fundamentals of distributed transactions and XA (two-phase commit) transactions
Configure an Apache Camel route with a Spring-managed JTA transaction manager
Set up two H2 data sources participating in a single distributed transaction
Trigger and verify both a successful commit and a forced rollback across both data sources
Troubleshoot common distributed transaction configuration issues
Prerequisites
Before starting this lab, you should have:

Basic understanding of Apache Camel concepts (routes, processors, components)
Familiarity with Java programming and Maven build tool
Knowledge of basic SQL and JDBC concepts
Understanding of ACID properties and the two-phase commit protocol
Experience with basic Spring Framework configuration (XML bean definitions)
Environment Setup
This lab uses a Java/Maven environment. If you are working in the Al Nafi cloud machine, OpenJDK and Maven are already installed. If you are working in your own isolated practice environment, install the prerequisites with:

# Verify Java (11 or later required)
java -version

# Verify Maven (3.6 or later required)
mvn -version
If either command is missing, install OpenJDK 11 and Maven 3.8+ using your operating system's package manager before continuing (for example, apt install openjdk-11-jdk maven on Debian/Ubuntu, or brew install openjdk@11 maven on macOS). No message broker installation is required for this lab â�� we keep the scope to JDBC-only XA transactions across two H2 databases, which is enough to demonstrate the full two-phase commit lifecycle without needing external services.

