Task 3: Implement and Verify Commit and Rollback Behavior
Step 3.1: Create the Route Builder
Create src/main/java/com/example/camel/DistributedTransactionRoutes.java. The route inserts a row into orders (orders database) and decrements inventory (inventory database) inside a single JTA-managed transaction. When simulateFailure is set on the message, an exception is thrown after the orders insert but before the inventory update completes, forcing a full rollback of both operations.

package com.example.camel;

import org.apache.camel.Exchange;
import org.apache.camel.LoggingLevel;
import org.apache.camel.Processor;
import org.apache.camel.builder.RouteBuilder;

import java.util.Map;

/**
 * Camel routes demonstrating a distributed (XA) transaction across two databases.
 */
public class DistributedTransactionRoutes extends RouteBuilder {

    @Override
    public void configure() throws Exception {

        onException(Exception.class)
            .handled(true)
            .log(LoggingLevel.ERROR, "Transaction rolled back: ${exception.message}")
            .setHeader("transactionResult", constant("ROLLED_BACK"));

        from("direct:processOrder")
            .routeId("processOrderRoute")
            .transacted("required")
            .log("Processing order for ${header.customerName}: ${header.quantity} x ${header.productName}")
            .to("ordersSQL:INSERT INTO orders (customer_name, product_name, quantity, status) "
                + "VALUES (:#customerName, :#productName, :#quantity, 'PROCESSING')")
            .process(new FailureInjector())
            .to("inventorySQL:UPDATE inventory SET available_quantity = available_quantity - :#quantity "
                + "WHERE product_name = :#productName AND available_quantity >= :#quantity")
            .process(exchange -> {
                if (exchange.getIn().getHeader("transactionResult") == null) {
                    exchange.getIn().setHeader("transactionResult", "COMMITTED");
                }
            })
            .log("Transaction result: ${header.transactionResult}");
    }

    /**
     * Throws an exception mid-route when the "simulateFailure" header is true,
     * to demonstrate that both the orders insert and inventory update are rolled back.
     */
    public static class FailureInjector implements Processor {
        @Override
        public void process(Exchange exchange) throws Exception {
            Boolean simulateFailure = exchange.getIn().getHeader("simulateFailure", Boolean.class);
            if (Boolean.TRUE.equals(simulateFailure)) {
                throw new RuntimeException("Simulated failure to trigger XA rollback");
            }
        }
    }
}
Step 3.2: Create the Application Entry Point
Create src/main/java/com/example/camel/XATransactionApplication.java. This class starts the Spring/Camel context, sends one successful order and one intentionally failing order through direct:processOrder, then queries both databases to show the resulting state before shutting down.

package com.example.camel;

import org.apache.camel.CamelContext;
import org.apache.camel.ProducerTemplate;
import org.springframework.context.support.ClassPathXmlApplicationContext;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

import javax.sql.DataSource;
import java.sql.Connection;
import java.sql.ResultSet;
import java.sql.Statement;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class XATransactionApplication {

    private static final Logger logger = LoggerFactory.getLogger(XATransactionApplication.class);

    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        logger.info("Starting Camel XA Transaction Application...");

        ClassPathXmlApplicationContext springContext =
            new ClassPathXmlApplicationContext("applicationContext.xml");

        CamelContext camelContext = springContext.getBean("camelContext", CamelContext.class);
        ProducerTemplate producerTemplate = camelContext.createProducerTemplate();

        // Scenario 1: successful order, should commit to both databases
        Map<String, Object> headers1 = new HashMap<>();
        headers1.put("customerName", "Alice Brown");
        headers1.put("productName", "Laptop");
        headers1.put("quantity", 2);
        headers1.put("simulateFailure", false);
        producerTemplate.sendBodyAndHeaders("direct:processOrder", "order-success", headers1);

        // Scenario 2: forced failure, both the order insert and inventory update must roll back
        Map<String, Object> headers2 = new HashMap<>();
        headers2.put("customerName", "Bob Johnson");
        headers2.put("productName", "Mouse");
        headers2.put("quantity", 3);
        headers2.put("simulateFailure", true);
        producerTemplate.sendBodyAndHeaders("direct:processOrder", "order-failure", headers2);

        // Verify final state in both databases
        DataSource ordersDataSource = springContext.getBean("ordersXADataSource", DataSource.class);
        DataSource inventoryDataSource = springContext.getBean("inventoryXADataSource", DataSource.class);

        printResults(ordersDataSource, "SELECT id, customer_name, product_name, quantity, status FROM orders", "ORDERS TABLE");
        printResults(inventoryDataSource, "SELECT product_name, available_quantity FROM inventory", "INVENTORY TABLE");

        camelContext.stop();
        springContext.close();
        logger.info("Application shutdown complete.");
    }

    private static void printResults(DataSource dataSource, String query, String label) throws Exception {
        logger.info("---- {} ----", label);
        try (Connection connection = dataSource.getConnection();
             Statement statement = connection.createStatement();
             ResultSet resultSet = statement.executeQuery(query)) {

            int columnCount = resultSet.getMetaData().getColumnCount();
            while (resultSet.next()) {
                StringBuilder row = new StringBuilder();
                for (int i = 1; i <= columnCount; i++) {
                    row.append(resultSet.getMetaData().getColumnName(i))
                       .append("=")
                       .append(resultSet.getString(i))
                       .append(" ");
                }
                logger.info(row.toString());
            }
        }
    }
}
Step 3.3: Build and Run the Application
mvn clean compile
mvn exec:java
Deliverable and expected output: In the console log, you should see, in order:

A log line Processing order for Alice Brown: 2 x Laptop followed by Transaction result: COMMITTED.
A log line Processing order for Bob Johnson: 3 x Mouse followed by Transaction rolled back: Simulated failure to trigger XA rollback.
Under ---- ORDERS TABLE ----, exactly one row for Alice Brown with status=PROCESSING, and no row for Bob Johnson (confirming the insert was rolled back along with the inventory update).
Under ---- INVENTORY TABLE ----, Laptop reduced from 10 to 8, and Mouse still at its original value of 50 (confirming the decrement for Bob Johnson's order never took effect).
Step 3.4: Confirm Rollback Atomicity
Re-run mvn exec:java a second time. Because the H2 databases are in-memory (DB_CLOSE_DELAY=-1) and recreated fresh by the Atomikos-managed data sources on each JVM start, you should see the same starting state (Laptop=10) and the same result pattern each run. This repeatability is your concrete check that the rollback path leaves no partial writes in either database â�� the defining guarantee of a correctly configured XA transaction.

Expected Outcomes
A working Maven project that starts an Atomikos-backed JTA transaction manager coordinating two independent H2 data sources through Apache Camel's SQL component.
Console output demonstrating that a successful order commits changes to both the orders and inventory tables atomically.
Console output demonstrating that a forced mid-route failure rolls back changes in both databases together, with no partial commit in either.
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: mvn exec:java fails with NoSuchBeanDefinitionException or a similar Spring bean lookup error. Diagnostic hint: check that the bean id used in getBean(...) calls inside XATransactionApplication.java exactly matches the id attribute defined in applicationContext.xml. Spring bean names are case-sensitive and must match exactly.

Issue 2: The application logs an XA-related exception mentioning "resource" or "unique resource name" during startup. Diagnostic hint: Atomikos requires every uniqueResourceName (ordersDB, inventoryDB) to be distinct across the entire JVM. If you re-run the application without fully stopping a previous instance, or copy this configuration into a project with another XA data source already using the same name, Atomikos will refuse to register the duplicate resource.

Conclusion
In this lab, you built a complete Apache Camel application that coordinates a genuine distributed transaction across two independent H2 databases using the Atomikos JTA transaction manager and Spring's transaction integration. You configured XA-capable data sources, wired them into Camel's SQL component, and implemented a route that performs an insert and an update as a single atomic unit of work. By running both a successful order and a deliberately failing order through the same route, you directly observed the two-phase commit protocol in action: a successful transaction committed changes to both databases, while a failure rolled back changes in both databases together, leaving no partial state. This is the core guarantee that distinguishes distributed (XA) transactions from independent, uncoordinated local transactions. As a next step, consider extending this lab to use real standalone database servers (such as PostgreSQL or MySQL with XA driver support) instead of in-memory H2 instances, or reintroducing a JMS broker as a third XA resource to see how Camel and Atomikos coordinate commit and rollback across three participants instead of two.
